WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.480
On the afternoon of
Thanksgiving 1962,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.330
I decided to start project MAC.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.210
And the next day I
had an appointment

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.540
with the provost,
Charlie Townes.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.390
And I told him
what I had in mind,

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.630
and I said, well, think
about it and let me know.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:24.470
And he said, oh, no.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.400
Go ahead.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.280
So encouraged by that
during the weekend,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.550
I wrote a little
memorandum-- two pages.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.220
Then on Monday I did
[INAUDIBLE] around MIT,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.830
and on Tuesday, I went to see
the president, J. Stratton.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.170
And the only question
that he had--

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.590
where are you going to do it?

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.640
And by that time, I
had done some homework

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.230
and find out that the new
building that was being

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.990
built in Technology Square.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.190
So there was a
space on Thursday.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.200
Licklider was supposed to come
to MIT for something else,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.640
so everybody got together
in the president's office.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.931
We shook hands, and that was it.

00:01:12.931 --> 00:01:13.430
Oh, wow.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.665
Exactly one week.

00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:17.540
There were no computer
scientists, after all,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.500
because nobody had a
computer science program yet.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.380
So everybody was a
mathematician, a physicist,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.960
an electrical
engineer, a drop out.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.550
We had them all.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.940
But they had skipped
the analog age.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.920
Every one of them was peculiar,
and different, and unusual,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:43.160
and maybe artistic, but
whatever they wanted to do

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.850
they would get done.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.820
The project that
had to be done was

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.460
whatever was the
right thing to do

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.705
because I discovered the
right path, and I liked that.

00:01:53.705 --> 00:01:55.820
That's the way
science ought to be.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.610
There had been an initial
surge to try to do something

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.110
about timesharing.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.970
So I decided to initiate
basically a quick hack

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:11.150
by creating a timesharing
system on using the IBM 709,

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.680
but by just basically
using the mechanisms that

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.650
had been tacked onto the 709 so
that the operating system could

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.420
begin to function as
a time sharing system,

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.990
we basically took
a very quick cut

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.970
at creating what
came to be called

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.630
the compatible
timesharing system.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.120
The hackers worked
on their computers,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.580
but there was all the CTSS,
and the [? many folks, ?]

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.110
and others.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.100
The hackers felt
like, by golly, we

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.830
got to be able to do
better than they did.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:51.530
So [INAUDIBLE] initial operating
system was called CTSS,

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.440
and so they called there's ITS.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.980
CTSS is compatible
timesharing system,

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.910
and ITS was the incompatible
timesharing system, and that

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:06.410
created a culture
among the hackers,

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:07.940
which was really hard to beat.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.100
There was wonderful family.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:15.110
Several years afterwards
when Project MAC

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:22.572
was really become a laboratory,
I tried to change the name.

00:03:22.572 --> 00:03:25.740
The administration
wouldn't let me.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:33.560
The laboratory was a piece of
a department doing research

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:39.310
into an established area,
and computer science

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:43.040
was regarded as an
established area.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.090
One of the things we did towards
the beginning of Project MAC

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:50.640
was we decided we had to
revise, revamp, and redo

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.740
the file system.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:56.120
That created different
new, unknown atmosphere

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.930
in Project MAC.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.480
That is, the computer
system became

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:10.070
a property of the people,
and this was a phenomenon

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.290
that we didn't expect.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:16.310
People tried--
created a community,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.450
and people started writing
a program for other people

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.390
to use, which was a new thing.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:25.310
We had a big memory--

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.960
a whole megabyte of RAM.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.290
In my pocket is 10,000
times as much RAM

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.960
as Joel had-- as the biggest
computer we had then,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.150
but we were on the
leading edge saying

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.250
we need that sort of
thing, and that became

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.402
the standard sort of thing.

00:04:38.402 --> 00:04:40.610
And I don't think if we
weren't doing that, that that

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.560
wouldn't have happened.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.400
We all knew about Moore's law,
and I sort of believed it,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.430
but I can't for the
life of me figure out

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:54.050
why I couldn't have
anticipated what's happened.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.675
I just didn't, and I
don't know whether that

00:04:56.675 --> 00:05:02.030
was myopia or what, but
it's all so logical when

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:07.080
you look backwards, and it seems
to happen just on schedule,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.430
but why didn't we predict it?

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:11.210
I don't know.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.390
Well, I think the kinds
of things we did back then

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:18.170
have become part of
mainstream computer science,

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:21.020
and as they become
more important,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.630
they become less conspicuous.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.760
People were shocked.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.470
I mean, there were two things.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:32.810
One was the idea that
out of a typewriter

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.790
you could control the computer.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:41.105
This was basically
unthinkable for many people.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:47.890
And then the powering
added to the thing--

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:48.640
what you could do.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:57.790
It was just a different world
that people were enchanted.

